                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-content-section">
        <div class="article-content">
            <div class="article-cover">
                <img alt="AI agent activity flowing through observability signals to a human review console" class="article-cover__image" width="1600" height="1067" src="/static/images/continuous-agent-monitoring.avif">
            </div>

            <div class="article-body">
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    Most conversations about secure AI begin before deployment. Teams define approved data sources, limit tool access, add refusal rules, test representative cases, and require human approval for sensitive decisions. Those controls are necessary, but they answer only one question: how should the system work?
                </p>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    Production creates a second question: how will the organization know whether those controls continue to work across unfamiliar inputs, changing permissions, accumulated context, and thousands of decisions? An agent can remain inside its technical permissions and still make a poor decision. A human can click approve without conducting meaningful review. A workflow can drift gradually without producing one obvious failure.
                </p>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    This distinction is central to <em>The Observability Gap in AI Agents: What to Fix Before Agents Scale</em>, a new <a class="article-link article-link--underlined" href="https://partnershiponai.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Partnership on AI</a> report. The report also carries a personal connection for me: one of its co-authors, <a class="article-link article-link--underlined" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/eric-mibuari-phd-bb24044/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Eric Mibuari, PhD</a>, and I were high school classmates. He worked with a multidisciplinary group of AI practitioners and researchers to evaluate what enterprises need to monitor and where leading agent frameworks still leave important telemetry gaps.
                </p>

                <div class="article-note">
                    <p class="article-note__title">The practical distinction</p>
                    <p class="article-note__text">
                        Guardrails define intended behavior. Observability records what happened. Continuous assurance turns those records into signals that help people detect risk, intervene, and review decisions after the fact.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <h2 class="section-heading section-heading--spaced">Testing cannot cover production reality</h2>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    Traditional software testing works best when inputs and outcomes can be bounded. AI agents complicate that model. They interpret requests, select tools, retrieve information, delegate work, revise plans, and act in environments that change after testing is complete. The number of possible paths grows quickly.
                </p>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    Pre-deployment evaluation should therefore be treated as the beginning of assurance, not its conclusion. Runtime monitoring is what gives an organization a chance to identify behavior that a finite test suite did not anticipate.
                </p>

                <h2 class="section-heading section-heading--spaced">Continuous monitoring is not a new operating model</h2>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    Continuous agent monitoring extends an operating discipline Tabiri already knows through cybersecurity. In our <a class="article-link article-link--underlined" href="/case-studies#cdp-mapsync">work with CDP-MapSync</a>, a network-sensor pilot grew into ongoing endpoint visibility, practical remediation, and human-governed investigation. The value came not from collecting every possible event, but from establishing a baseline, identifying meaningful deviations, assigning owners, and connecting alerts to action.
                </p>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    The operating principles transfer, but the signals do not map one for one. Cybersecurity monitoring focuses on systems, identities, vulnerabilities, and network or endpoint activity. Agent assurance must also examine what an agent was authorized to do, which tools and evidence shaped its action, whether delegation expanded its authority, how its behavior changed, and whether a person could intervene effectively. A security information and event management platform can contribute useful telemetry, but it is not, by itself, an agent assurance program.
                </p>

                <h2 class="section-heading section-heading--spaced">Six questions a production monitoring program should answer</h2>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    The report organizes enterprise monitoring needs around six goals. They provide a useful operating checklist for any team preparing to scale an agent.
                </p>

                <div class="article-feature-grid article-feature-grid--two-columns">
                    <article class="trust-card-no-hover">
                        <div class="card-heading-row">
                            <div class="icon-tile icon-tile--inline"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">policy</span></div>
                            <h3 class="heading-medium heading-medium--primary">Did it follow policy?</h3>
                        </div>
                        <p class="body-copy-small">Detect outputs or actions that violate enterprise rules, legal requirements, data-handling restrictions, or expected refusal behavior.</p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="trust-card-no-hover">
                        <div class="card-heading-row">
                            <div class="icon-tile icon-tile--inline"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">lock</span></div>
                            <h3 class="heading-medium heading-medium--primary">Was the action authorized?</h3>
                        </div>
                        <p class="body-copy-small">Check the tools, data, operations, and permission mode in force when the agent acted.</p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="trust-card-no-hover">
                        <div class="card-heading-row">
                            <div class="icon-tile icon-tile--inline"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">gpp_good</span></div>
                            <h3 class="heading-medium heading-medium--primary">Was human review meaningful?</h3>
                        </div>
                        <p class="body-copy-small">Confirm that the right person reviewed the decision with enough time and authority to approve, stop, or redirect it.</p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="trust-card-no-hover">
                        <div class="card-heading-row">
                            <div class="icon-tile icon-tile--inline"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">monitoring</span></div>
                            <h3 class="heading-medium heading-medium--primary">Has behavior changed?</h3>
                        </div>
                        <p class="body-copy-small">Identify drift in escalation, refusal, source use, quality, or other behavior that matters to the workflow.</p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="trust-card-no-hover">
                        <div class="card-heading-row">
                            <div class="icon-tile icon-tile--inline"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">account_tree</span></div>
                            <h3 class="heading-medium heading-medium--primary">Did delegation expand risk?</h3>
                        </div>
                        <p class="body-copy-small">Trace authority, context, and evidence across agent handoffs so delegation does not silently widen access.</p>
                    </article>

                    <article class="trust-card-no-hover">
                        <div class="card-heading-row">
                            <div class="icon-tile icon-tile--inline"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">fact_check</span></div>
                            <h3 class="heading-medium heading-medium--primary">Can the decision be reconstructed?</h3>
                        </div>
                        <p class="body-copy-small">Preserve a trustworthy record of inputs, sources, actions, handoffs, approvals, and outcomes for later review.</p>
                    </article>
                </div>

                <h2 class="section-heading section-heading--spaced">Logs are not the same as assurance</h2>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    A tool-call log can show that an agent opened a file. It cannot, by itself, show whether that agent was permitted to open the file for that case. A record of approval cannot show whether the reviewer had the right authority or whether approvals are becoming automatic. Telemetry becomes useful only when it is connected to organizational context.
                </p>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    That means joining agent activity to identity and access systems, tracing claims back to evidence, comparing outputs with policy or source material, and looking for patterns over time. The intent layer still belongs to the organization: policies, roles, deny lists, escalation rules, and definitions of unacceptable behavior must be explicit before monitoring can judge against them.
                </p>

                <blockquote class="article-quote">
                    <p class="article-quote__text">A record tells you what happened. Continuous assurance helps determine whether it should have happened and what to do next.</p>
                </blockquote>

                <h2 class="section-heading section-heading--spaced">Human-in-the-loop needs its own monitoring</h2>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    Human approval is often presented as the final safeguard, but the presence of an approval button does not prove meaningful oversight. Reviewers can become over-reliant on recommendations, approve too quickly, or lack the authority required for the decision.
                </p>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    Teams should be able to examine who reviewed a recommendation, what they decided, how long the review took, whether they sought more information, and how often they overrode the system. Those patterns help distinguish earned trust from rubber-stamping.
                </p>

                <h2 class="section-heading section-heading--spaced">What organizations can do now</h2>
                <ol class="article-list article-list--checklist">
                    <li><strong class="article-emphasis">Start with one bounded workflow.</strong> Name the decisions, tools, data sources, handoffs, and people involved.</li>
                    <li><strong class="article-emphasis">Work backward from failure.</strong> Identify the policy violations, unauthorized actions, oversight failures, and drift that would matter most.</li>
                    <li><strong class="article-emphasis">Define identity and permissions.</strong> Give each agent a durable identity and make approval-mode changes visible.</li>
                    <li><strong class="article-emphasis">Capture evidence proportionately.</strong> Use stronger records and alerts for high-impact, difficult-to-reverse actions; use lighter monitoring for routine work.</li>
                    <li><strong class="article-emphasis">Connect signals to intervention.</strong> Assign owners, thresholds, escalation paths, and post-incident review before launch.</li>
                </ol>

                <h2 class="section-heading section-heading--spaced">Do not confuse visible reasoning with reliable evidence</h2>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced">
                    The report draws an important boundary between records of what an agent did and records of how a model reached its decision. Actions, permissions, memory changes, sources, and approvals are concrete operational records. Chain-of-thought summaries and confidence statements are less reliable and may not reflect the factors that actually drove the model.
                </p>
                <p class="body-copy-spaced body-copy-spaced--extended">
                    For practical governance, organizations should begin with evidence they can verify: identity, authority, tool activity, source attribution, human intervention, and outcomes. Explainability claims should not outrun what the system can faithfully observe.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
